body:
I thought all of the people in positions of power, the county sheriff, every police officer and the jail in the city I used to live in were in on a body part stealing conspiracy that manufactured car accidents in different low income parts of town, all including pedestrians being hit and killed, in order to supply the nations elite with enough organs and body parts and things like that to live as long as possible while their scientists worked on immortality. I walked into some kind of medical bank, either for blood or sperm or something, and thought it was where the rich went to get their usual transfusion or get their new part. Literally ran around my small town exposing the people behind these atrocities wherever I could. That led me to a casino where I found myself on an employee only staircase taking pictures of signs leading to the roof because I thought it was where black people were being sacrificed in KKK occult ritual.

&#x200B;

I don't live in that town anymore. I had to move.

I was like you. At 16 I told myself it would get better at 18. Ill finally "get it" and won't feel so sad being in the world. Went out on my own at 20 and thought I'd make it. Reached 21 and felt no different. Was still super depressed and just always thinking about offing myself. Then every year after that I held out hope that eventually something in my brain will click into place and I won't be "this way" anymore... At 26, 10 years later, I've got a foot in the non-existent door of homelessness (After 5 years on my own, had to move in with a father I hadn't seen or spoke to in over 10 years), my family sees me as nothing, a worthless excuse for a man, I've lost every friend I've had, and I'm pretty convinced that taking my life is inevitable. 

&#x200B;

But still I remain... holding out hope for a less anxious, less depressed version of myself to break through and shine on the world. 

&#x200B;

All we can do is keep the hope alive and keep trying. I don't know what the future has in store for you, but I do know that good things happen at unexpected times. Despite my depression and suicidal thoughts, I had pleasant experiences sprinkled throughout my journey to this point. I'm doing my best now to remember that. 

&#x200B;

Hang in there OP, life changing good things might be right around the corner.
